"Summary for a benefit or form
A summary to help with a benefit claim or form.
Sam Taylor
Example: fall at work (made up)
1 June 2026
What happened
When and where
1 February 2026 · 14:20 · Loading bay, Northgate Distribution, Leeds
Beforehand
I was unloading pallets at the end of a normal shift.
What happened
A pallet slipped off the forklift and knocked me down. I landed on my right wrist and hit my head on the kerb.
What happened next
A first aider stayed with me and my supervisor called an ambulance. I was taken to Leeds General Infirmary.
Complications
The wrist needed an operation to fix it with a plate two weeks later.
What I was told at the time
The paramedic said I might have broken my wrist and should not drive.
Other people there
Priya from the day shift, and my supervisor Dan.
Police, ambulance or fire service
Ambulance. The incident was logged in the accident book.
Injuries and symptoms
Broken right wrist (distal radius). Concussion. Bruised hip.
How it affects me
Preparing food
How it is
It is harder now
What happens
I can’t chop or lift a full pan. I mostly use ready meals.
Help I need
Someone to lift pans and drain vegetables.
How often
Every day
Using the toilet
How it is
It is harder now
What happens
Cleaning myself with my left hand is difficult and slow.
How often
Every day
Dressing and undressing
How it is
I find this very difficult
What happens
I can’t do buttons or zips with my right hand. My partner helps me every morning.
Help I need
Someone to help with buttons, zips and socks.
Aids or equipment
A sock aid and elastic shoelaces.
How often
Every day
Doing it safely, and more than once
I lose my balance putting trousers on, so I sit down.
The time it takes
About twice as long as before.
Doing it properly, to a good standard
I often have to leave shirts undone.
Mixing with other people
How it is
It is harder now
What happens
I avoid busy places because I worry someone will knock my arm.
How often
Most days
Anything else this has changed
I had to stop playing five-a-side football, which was my main way of seeing friends.
Treatment and medication
Straight afterwards
X-ray, a plaster cast and painkillers in A&E.
Ongoing care
Physiotherapy weekly, and fracture clinic every six weeks.
